d show that uniform post-history tail conditions are too strong even at positive payoff scale, and would motivate expectation, quantile, or predictable-tail alternatives."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"These quantities are horizon-dependent and may still be far from necessary for learnability; they may mostly improve sufficiency rather than characterize possibility.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","comparison inequalities","counterexample calculations"],"summary":"Develop the hierarchy between Delta_T(H), the smaller comparator gap Gamma_T(H)=sup_a V_T(a)-sup_a V_T^H(a), and possible argmax-stability gaps. Prove the clean regret comparison R_T <= R_T^H + Gamma_T(H), compute Gamma on CE-001/CE-003/CE-005, and identify when Gamma_T(H_T)=o(T) is enough for truncation-based algorithms.","title":"Sharper Truncation Gap Hierarchy","why_promising":"It gives an immediate sharper sufficient condition and may reveal exactly which part of delayed payoff mass matters to fixed-comparator regret."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most cited algorithms are bandit or OCO results, while the market-making reduction is full-information with arm-dependent and infinite non-fill delays; the mapping may require a new wrapper rather than direct citation.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature mapping","model reduction"],"summary":"Translate adaptive-delay and skipping algorithms to the finite grid market-making model. Treat unresolved fills after a chosen cutoff as skipped observations and aim for regret bounded by learning terms plus skipped payoff mass, without knowing beta, gamma, or a good H in advance. Compare residue-class Hedge, Joulani-Gyorgy-Szepesvari reductions, Gyorgy-Joulani skipping, and optimism-with-delay methods.","title":"Adaptive Skipping For Grid Market Making","why_promising":"It could turn the sufficient theorem into a more usable algorithm and connect predictable long delays to optimism rather than worst-case truncation loss."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Advers