-The main unresolved issue is quantifiers. A true necessity theorem must specify whether "possible" means instance-wise existence of a process-aware learner, a universal learner over a process class, or a minimax/distribution-free guarantee. The promising necessity route is no longer raw delay-tail mass, but an information-theoretic condition: unresolved delayed payoff mass must be unpredictable and necessary to identify the best fixed quote.
+The main unresolved issue is quantifiers. A true necessity theorem must specify whether "possible" means instance-wise existence of a process-aware learner, a horizon-oblivious process-aware learner, a universal learner over a process class, a minimax/distribution-free guarantee, or a post-history conditional guarantee. The best current lower-bound route is class-uniform/minimax: in a finite block \(0^N c\), \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\), every randomized learner suffers regret at least \(N/4\) on one continuation because the two continuations are indistinguishable before the terminal price. Concatenating dominant blocks may yield a universal-class impossibility, but it is not an instance-wise characterization.
