working_notes/literature_map.md:5:- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (ICML/PMLR 2013) is the primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction. Verified source states the ICML 2013/PMLR venue and frames delay effects on regret; use this for the finite-grid delayed-learning step, after explicitly mapping public price observations to delayed full-information reward vectors.
working_notes/literature_map.md:6:- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (AAAI 2016), "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization," is verified. The source states a black-box reduction from delayed-feedback to standard online learning for full-information OCO and adaptive-gradient algorithms. Useful for horizon-free/adaptive variants of the grid reduction.
working_notes/literature_map.md:8:- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference. It was reported by agents but not source-verified in this synthesis; keep as background candidate.
working_notes/literature_map.md:18:- Gyorgy and Joulani (ICML/PMLR 2021) is now verified. It gives adversarial bandit bounds adapting to observed delays/data and includes skip-set bounds. Relevant for replacing a fixed truncation horizon by adaptive skipping; not directly full-information market making.
working_notes/literature_map.md:19:- Flaspohler et al. (ICML/PMLR 2021) is now verified. It reduces delayed online learning to optimism and may help treat predictable delayed fills as hints rather than worst-case lost mass.
working_notes/literature_map.md:25:- Duetting, Guruganesh, Schneider, and Wang (ICML/PMLR 2023) is now verified. It studies one-sided Lipschitz maximization, motivated by pricing/contract design, and gives very small regret in that setting. Potential tool for threshold reward geometry and improved discretization, but delayed/adversarial time variation may block direct application.
working_notes/literature_map.md:26:- Kleinberg and Leighton (FOCS 2003) is verified through DOI/DBLP metadata and accessible technical-report text. It is foundational posted-price regret background and gives lower-bound context for threshold/pricing geometry.
working_notes/literature_map.md:31:- Yao (FOCS 1977) is verified through DOI/IEEE/DBLP metadata. It is relevant background for distributional-vs-randomized lower-bound framing, although the finite two-continuation block proof here is elementary averaging and does not require invoking the full principle.
working_notes/literature_map.md:37:- Pike-Burke, Agrawal, Szepesvari, and Grunewalder (ICML 2018/arXiv candidate) on delayed aggregated anonymous feedback remains unchecked; likely background rather than direct support.
